PyVISA-py Changelog
===================

0.9.0 (unreleased)
------------------

- ``VI_ATTR_SEND_END_EN`` is honored on a VXI-11 write. The END indicator
  was always requested for the last block regardless of the attribute.
- ``viLock`` on a VXI-11 resource now asks the instrument to wait, and waits
  for the caller's timeout rather than a fixed ten seconds. Without the
  waitlock flag a conformant instrument refuses at once, so the timeout could
  never elapse. ``VI_ATTR_RSRC_LOCK_STATE`` is also reported, as VPP-4.3
  RULE 3.6.2 requires of every resource. Closes #583
- The socket deadline for a VXI-11 call now allows for the timeouts the call
  itself carries. ``viLock`` and ``viOpen`` send a lock timeout the instrument
  is entitled to spend, but the socket gave up after a fixed five seconds, so
  a socket error escaped in place of the instrument's answer. Closes #583
- A VXI-11 link that the instrument refuses is now reported as a VISA error
  from ``viOpen`` rather than a bare ``Exception``, and the TCP connection is
  closed instead of being left open.
- VXI-11 error codes that are not in the conversion table no longer raise
  KeyError out of the VISA call. They are reported as VI_ERROR_IO. Error 21,
  invalid address, is defined by VXI-11 B.5.2 and was missing from the table;
  it is now mapped to VI_ERROR_RSRC_NFOUND.
- A VXI-11 instrument that closes the connection is now reported as
  VI_ERROR_CONN_LOST straight away. A closed socket stays ready for select,
  so the receive loop ran at full CPU until the VISA timeout expired and then
  reported a timeout.
- Opening a VXI-11 resource whose instrument is unreachable now fails with a
  VISA error rather than BrokenPipeError. A refused connection makes the
  socket ready for select, which was taken for a completed connection, so the
  failure only surfaced on the first send.
- A HiSLIP instrument that closes the connection is now reported as
  ``VI_ERROR_CONN_LOST``, and other socket failures as ``VI_ERROR_IO``,
  rather than letting a ``RuntimeError`` or ``OSError`` escape the VISA call.
- Bring HiSLIP (TCPIP::INSTR) sessions to parity with VXI-11 and GPIB.
  ``viLock``/``viUnlock`` (exclusive and shared), ``viAssertTrigger``,
  ``viGpibControlREN`` and ``viFlush`` are implemented, along with SRQ events
  through both ``wait_on_event`` and ``install_handler``.
  ``VI_ATTR_TERMCHAR``/``VI_ATTR_TERMCHAR_EN`` are honored on read,
  ``VI_ATTR_SEND_END_EN`` on write, and ``VI_ATTR_RSRC_LOCK_STATE`` reports
  the session lock state. Trigger, lock and remote/local map dropped
  connections to ``VI_ERROR_CONN_LOST`` and other socket failures to
  ``VI_ERROR_IO``, as read and write already do.
- The HiSLIP asynchronous channel is now read by a dedicated thread that
  demultiplexes messages. An ``AsyncServiceRequest`` or ``AsyncInterrupted``
  arriving during an unrelated exchange was previously taken for its response.
- Fixed HiSLIP ``open_timeout``, which was accepted and then ignored, and
  scaled as though it were in seconds when it is in milliseconds.
- A HiSLIP read now reports the status VPP-4.3 defines for it. A read the
  DataEND message stopped is ``VI_SUCCESS``, since RULE 6.1.1 ranks END above
  both the termination character and the byte count; it reported
  ``VI_SUCCESS_TERM_CHAR`` at every message end, although no termination
  character was honored on read at all. ``VI_SUCCESS_TERM_CHAR`` now means
  what RULE 6.1.2 says, a read a termination character stopped with no END,
  and bytes past that character are held back for the next read rather than
  discarded. Behavior change for code inspecting the status.
- The HiSLIP RMT-delivered flag and message id are now claimed atomically.
  They are shared by the synchronous send path and by ``AsyncStatusQuery``, so
  a status query racing a write could deliver the flag twice or lose it, and
  the instrument answered the next command with "-410 Query INTERRUPTED".
- ``device_clear`` discards data left in the HiSLIP synchronous channel and
  resets all message-tracking state rather than only the message id; a stale
  message could desynchronize the next read.
  ``hislip.DEVICE_CLEAR_SETTLE_TIME`` can be raised for instruments needing
  longer than 100 ms to work through in-process messages.
- A HiSLIP lock request that times out client side now speculatively releases
  the lock, so one granted late by the server is not held until the session is
  closed.
- ``viTerminate`` no longer waits a fixed two seconds for an ``Interrupted``
  message many HiSLIP instruments never send; the device clear handshake skips
  over interleaved messages instead.
- A HiSLIP ``Error`` or ``FatalError`` from the server is reported instead of
  being flushed as unrecognized traffic. It is the only way a server can
  report a refused transaction, so the caller previously saw
  ``VI_ERROR_TIMEOUT`` with no diagnostic. Both channels now raise
  ``hislip.HiSLIPServerError``, carrying the server's own explanation, mapped
  to ``VI_ERROR_IO`` (``VI_ERROR_CONN_LOST`` for a ``FatalError``). Control
  codes in the device-defined range 128-255 no longer raise ``KeyError``.
- Fixed HiSLIP conformance issues found by cross-checking against IVI-6.1
  revision 2.0. ``AsyncStatusQuery``, ``AsyncLock`` release and
  ``AsyncRemoteLocalControl`` carried the id the client would send *next*
  rather than the most recently sent one (6.14, 6.5), which had conformant
  servers report MAV false on every ``viReadSTB``. An unrecognized message is
  now answered with ``Error`` and a framing failure with ``FatalError`` on
  both channels (6.3, 6.2), the client waits for an ``AsyncInterrupted``
  before sending again (3.1.2 rule 4), buffered server messages are discarded
  when the client sends (3.1.2 rule 3), and ``InitializeResponse`` reads only
  bit 0 of the control code as the overlap flag (6.1) rather than mistaking a
  server announcing encryption for one requesting overlapped mode.
- Set the TCP keepalive probe timers defensively for all TCPIP resources.
  ``VI_ATTR_TCPIP_KEEPALIVE`` raised ``AttributeError`` on platforms without
  ``socket.TCP_KEEPIDLE``, which includes macOS.
- TCPIP::SOCKET can now also be found via `list_resources(query='TCPIP?*')` #622 PR #623
- Apply ``open_timeout`` to the connection attempt across all the TCP
  transports, as VPP-4.3 PERMISSION 4.3.2 allows. An ``open_timeout`` of 0
  (``VI_TMO_IMMEDIATE``) now means 2000 ms, which is what VPP-4.3
  RECOMMENDATION 4.3.3 asks of an implementation that does this.
  ``ResourceManager.open_resource`` passes 0 whenever the caller supplies no
  ``open_timeout``, so VXI-11 opens had been allowing 100 ms for the whole TCP
  handshake and reporting anything slower as ``VI_ERROR_RSRC_NFOUND``. HiSLIP
  now honors ``open_timeout`` rather than ignoring it. That same 2000 ms is the
  new default everywhere, replacing 5 s for VXI-11 and HiSLIP and 10 s for
  ``TCPIP::SOCKET``. The trade-off is that ``VI_TMO_IMMEDIATE`` can no longer
  mean "never wait on a lock". Use the session's ``lock_timeout`` for that. See
  the FAQ. Closes #578
- VXI-11: Allow link ID 0 to be used for SRQ in agreement with the specs
  and other implementations. Closes #618 PR #619
- Removed return packet on device_intr_srq. Closes #614 PR #615
- Removed SRQ server shutdown if the server was already shut down, reducing potential problems with non compliant devices. Closes #609 PR #610
- Removed additional read_stb inside VXI-11 (TCPIP::INSTR) SRQ handling.
  It is not standard behaviour, may confuse instruments, and does not
  cache the SRQ bit as would be required. Closes #603 PR #606
- Added read_stb, assert_trigger and gpib_control_run functions to usbtmc PR #532
- Implement viTerminate() for HiSLIP sessions using a CancellableSocket
  (self-pipe trick on recv_into), enabling cross-thread I/O cancellation
  without destroying the session. Also handle HiSLIP Interrupted messages
  in the receive path. Closes #566
- Fixed VXI-11 read timeouthandling. When reads were splitdown into multiple read 
  chunks the timeout value for each chunk was calculated wrongly. This resulted in 
  very high timeout values which had to be set.
  A second addressed issue is that timeout values never decrement to 0. A timeout value 
  of 0 is undefined in VXI-11 standard. It can mean "timeout immediately if no data 
  is in buffer" or "block permanently until transfer is finished".
- Implement the VISA event subsystem for VXI-11 (TCPIP::INSTR) resources:
  `viEnableEvent`, `viDisableEvent`, `viDiscardEvents`, `viWaitOnEvent`,
  `viInstallHandler`, and `viUninstallHandler`. SRQ (service request) events
  are now supported via both queue-based (`wait_on_event`) and handler-based
  (`install_handler`) delivery. A daemon thread runs an ONC RPC TCP interrupt
  server to receive VXI-11 `DEVICE_INTR_SRQ` callbacks. This also fixes the
  `create_intr_chan` XDR packer in `protocols/vxi11.py`.
  Other transports (GPIB, USBTMC, HiSLIP, Serial) remain unsupported for now. PR #577 
- Pass termchar information to USBTMC devices when reading PR #598
- Properly configure termchar on GPIB devices. This fix timeout issues for old GPIB devices which do not signal EOI PR #599
- Clean up GPIB calls to the ib*() routines, by using symbolic definitions
  rather than bare integers.  PR #601

0.8.1 (04-09-2025)
------------------

- allow users to easily change the VXI-11 lock timeout PR #496
- ensure listing of GPIB resources works after PR #484 PR #523
- add support for pyocd/libusb_package PyUSB backend PR #524

0.8.0 (01-04-2025)
------------------

- add support for USB and Ethernet Prologix adapters PR #484
- improve message when USB device serial number is not readable PR #423
- ignore network interface card with no subnet mask when discovering TCPIP
  resources PR #478
- add VICP INSTR to the supported interface for TCPIP keep alive attr PR #477
- add read_stb method for TCPIP HiSLIP client PR #429
- fix usbtmc implementation to respect section 3.3 of the spec PR #449
  Read now reads from usb until a "short packet" or if all data (`transfer_size`) PR #465
  has been read (see specification), and only expects a header on the first packet received.
- fix usbtmc implementation to properly discard the alignment bytes
  ensuring only the actual data (`transfer_size`) is retained in the message PR #465
- Implemented partial USBTMC message functionality that allows reading the amount of bytes
  specified by host PR #470
- add support for VI_ATTR_SUPPRESS_END_EN for USB resources PR #449
- support open_timeout for TCPIP hislip resources PR #430
- fix serial flow control configuration PR #483
- Fix large data transfer using USBTMC PR #490

